|> Exercise
    handle=_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_

    Show that
    $$
    \cos(\alpha - \beta) = \cos\alpha\cos\beta + \sin\alpha\sin\beta
    $$
    for all $\alpha$, $\beta \in \rr$
    via a geometric argument.
    (I.e., AVOID using the angle-sum formula.)

    |> Solution

        We know (or should know, or are about to
        know) (or are about to _believe_) that

        $$
        \cos(\alpha - \beta)
        $$

        is the projection of a unit vector of angle
        $\alpha$ onto a ruler of angle $\beta$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol1_v2.svg

        In what follows we will write ‘$\vecb{i}$’
        and ‘$\vecb{j}$’, respectively, for the vectors
        $(1, 0)$ and $(0, 1)$ (in the manner of physicists):

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol2.svg

        As established in Exercise >>_project_i_and_j_
        $\vecb{i}$ and $\vecb{j}$ project to $\cos\beta$,
        $\sin\beta$ respectively on the ruler: 

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol3_v2.svg

        On the other hand, the vector $(\cos\alpha, \sin\alpha)$
        is the sum of a scaled copy of $\vecb{i}$ and
        scaled copy of $\vecb{j}$,  specifically the sum of
        $\vecb{i}\cdot\cos(\alpha)$ and $\vecb{j}\cdot\sin(\alpha)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol5_v2.svg

        The scaled copy of $\vecb{i}$ projects to
        $\cos(\alpha)\cdot\cos(\beta)$ on the ruler, 
        by scaling the projection of $\vecb{i}$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol6_v2.svg

        Similarly, the scaled copy of $\vecb{j}$ projects
        to $\sin(\alpha)\cdot\sin(\beta)$, by scaling the
        projection of $\vecb{j}$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol7_v2.svg

        Moreover, the sum-of-projections is the
        projection-of-the-sum:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_unit_dot_product_sol8_v2.svg

        This gives us

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \cos(\alpha-\beta) = \cos\alpha\cos\beta + \sin\alpha\sin\beta
            $$

        since both sides compute the projection of
        $(\cos \alpha, \sin \alpha)$ onto the ruler of angle $\beta$.

